\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The Elvi guard escorted Sarah and Chanta into a jagged crevasse between two crumbling buildings, each standing only because the other threatened to collapse. Inside, the noise of merrymaking escalated, the crowd thickened, anticipation tightening with every uneasy step. Up ahead, Sarah spotted a shimmering light, vibrating with gladiatorial ecstasy, the thrill of life and death, set to music.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Almost there now.\'94 Lonnie pat Sarah\'92s elbow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alongside, Chanta muttered, his resentment of the Sages festering. \'93If they show their faces again, I\'92ll\'85\'94 He punched the solar plexus of an imaginary ghost.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They emerged under interrogation-strength lighting onto a muddy, half-kilometre-wide paddock, trampled by a horde of carousing Elvi. Every third ground floor was a bar, patrons lined up dozens deep for a drink. Where the crowd thinned, buskers set battered guitar cases and battery amps, belting out the familiar hits Sarah had heard in Ghetto karaoke bars.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta shadow-boxed another sage apparition, nearly clocking a stray Elvis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As Sarah and Chanta pressed deeper into the throng, the sound seized her\'97raw, feral, a pulse in her ribs that dared her heart to keep up. Bombast, sweat, and longing smashed together in a field of sound. Overhead, projection-mapped fireworks exploded, raining disco embers over a crowd packed tighter than sardines in a jukebox. Nearby, an Elvis in peacock blue surfed the crowd atop an inflatable white Cadillac. An Elvine in gold lam\'e9 launched herself onto the horde, body-surfing atop a plastic guitar and scattering leis and fake casino chips like confetti from a busted pi\'f1ata. Another Elvis, resplendent in a \'93Taking Care of Business\'94 suit, glided overhead on a giant flamingo pool toy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>This was as far from Airborne cocktail parties and curated cloud lounges as music could get. Here, even the sky had to fight to be heard. Sarah felt the vibration in her bones\'97music that refused transcendence, music that made you love the world\'92s fingerprints and bruises.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Underfoot, the ground sloped gently downward, and suddenly they were standing in a vast amphitheatre. Up ahead, the Graceland main stage resolved out of the lights, a spectacle worthy of every universe\'92s wildest dreams of rock-and-roll excess. It loomed, not so much a structure as a geological event; a slab of the Vegas strip wedged between crumbling buildings by a well-meaning, intoxicated god. Above, hologram palm trees swayed, light rigs and pyrotechnic cannons threatening the night sky with sunburn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The band glittered: a constellation of brass, woodwind, keyboards, guitars, percussion, and sequinned backup singers whose harmonies warbled somewhere between surf rock and spiritual warfare. Down front, Elvi in riotous jumpsuits the colours of highlighter pens and barely-legal energy drinks, grappled and lunged at each other. Pompadours bobbed, capes swung, and rhinestones flashed as fists, karate chops, and microphone blows blurred the line between choreography and vendetta.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why are they always fighting?\'94 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie hung her head, fiddling with her jacket tassels. \'93We can all agree on the music, sweetie. But never the lyrics. It\'92s like God\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93God?\'94 Sarah shot back, indignant. \'93Surely, you don\'92t\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'92Course not, sugar. But there\'92s a reason the old Grayling warranties said: never play gospel near us\'85 ELVIS units.\'94 Lonnie air-quoted with pure derision. \'93We channel the tunes, but the lyrics\'97no two Elvi think alike. You throw buskers or karaoke tragics in a room, and five drinks in, someone\'92s rhinestones are getting punched loose. Still, everyone settles down for the main show.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tonight, Sarah realised, the Elvi weren\'92t just defending lyrics or honour. They were protecting something wilder, unrulier, more fun than dignity itself. She grinned; she was exactly where she needed to be.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s that special?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s for our Momma,\'94 Lonnie said, more warmth in her voice than Sarah had ever heard from an Elvi.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah and Chanta were shown to a pair of colourful camp chairs, three-quarters back from the stage, where the mud was only ankle deep. Before they reached their seats, an Elvis face-planted in the mud at their feet, in blue Hawaii jumpsuit, gasping, blowing up like a street-corner party balloon.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Chanta froze mid-step, Sarah just behind him. Lonnie hissed, \'93Go! Use your epipen!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah fumbled for the pocket of her new jacket. \'93Wait\'85 what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Another Elvis in a velvet blazer dropped to his knees, flicked the cap off his own pen, jabbed it into Blue Hawaii\'92s thigh. Textbook. \'93You know how to use those, right?\'94 He nodded at Sarah\'92s pocket.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked. \'93Is\'85 is he okay?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Velvet Elvis checked Blue Hawaii\'92s bracelet. \'93Artificial pineapple. Only affects the Blue Hawaii series. Happens every year. You\'92d think\'85\'94 He shrugged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nobody is allergic to that!\'94 Sarah objected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stared. \'93But\'85 they are clones.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, well, Blue Hawaii series are allergic.\'94 Velvet Elvis didn\'92t smile. \'93Grayling left all sorts of ticking clocks in the \'91product\'92. Keeps things\'85 
\f2\i interesting
\f1\i0 .\'94 He helped Blue Hawaii to his feet, who shuddered and gave a thumbs-up. The party rolled on. Business as usual.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Emergency over, Sarah and Chanta took their seats. Before they\'92d even settled, a spotlight hit centre stage and an Elvis strolled to the mic\'97an Elvis who, by story\'92s end, would be known as the High Elvis.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

