\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 A fanfare of brass whirled around Sarah\'92s head, dizzy as ship\'92s bells. The sound of percussion churned and built\'97powering this voyage up the river Showbiz. Guitars, strings and woodwinds twisted and excited the air with licks and chords. The tension built until a familiar riff materialised, a tune that, in less mutant universes, would be known as 
\f2\i Polk Salad Annie.
\f1\i0  All around, the crowd bayed like ecstatic animals; they knew the tune. Then the lights detonated, and Sarah\'92s spirit spilled into pure colour and love. The beat yanked her back into her body; the rhythm entrained every cell, a white-water ride of overture and melody, no two seconds the same.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the second chorus, Sarah was out of her chair singing her own lyrics with the best of them. \'93Pokes at a Danny, Gator\'92s got your fanny,\'94 she sang.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta grabbed her and hugged her as he jiggled and rocked, laughter filling him up. Sarah didn\'92t mind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The song ended with the classiest trashcan outro, and the mob erupted with cheers, claps, a thunder of boots. Before Sarah could catch her breath, or her heart, the band had slammed into the next number.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Side-stage, a queue of Elvi snaked past the sound gear and downstairs, each taking their turn. Every number, a new vocalist. The acoustics were ridiculous: Sarah, dead centre in the amphitheatre, felt each note perfectly placed, reverb curling around her and warming the air. Three songs in, she looked up and around: balconies sagged with Elvi, dancing and lip-syncing, performing championship-level karate in blue and yellow sweeps of spotlight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A new song began, a shift in mood. Lights down. The martial artists froze, attentive. A soloist stepped forward to the mic. The tune was raw, bare. All truth. Sarah was overwhelmed by tears before the first chorus. She\'92d admired the earlier singers for their range, charisma, outsize machismo and reckless energy, and for their ludicrous karate moves. Now she sat, rapt: velvet voice, spun-sugar tremolo, the ache and poise in every phrase, every breath weighted just so.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For the first time, Sarah felt herself slip into a state of grace.  \'93
\f2\i Where I\'92ll be free to have an honest heart,
\f1\i0 \'94 she murmured, channeling improbably a French playwright not born in her universe. She felt loved. She was one filament in a never-ending chain, love passed through music, rhinestone, wrestling, song. Infinite, ceaseless, unstoppable love.
